= 1. Beginnings and Chronological Framework

The Indus Valley or Harappan Civilisation is dated between *c. 2600 and 1900 BCE* for its mature phase.
Archaeologists divide the regional cultural sequence into three distinct chronological horizons:

- *Early Harappan (c. 3000--2600 BCE):* Marked by distinct pottery styles, small villages, and limited craft production. There were no large cities. Pastoralism and small-scale farming sustained the communities.
- *Mature Harappan (c. 2600--1900 BCE):* The fully urban phase. Cities exhibited monumental brick architecture, uniform weights and measures, elaborate drainage networks, and specialized craft production.
- *Late Harappan (Post-1900 BCE):* Period of urban breakdown. Standardized artifacts vanished. Populations migrated eastward into Gujarat, Haryana, and western Uttar Pradesh.

The civilization is named after *Harappa*, the first site discovered in 1921.
Its distinctive cultural markers include steatite seals, baked bricks, chert blades, and stone beads.
Settlements stretched from *Sutkagen Dor* in Baluchistan to *Alamgirpur* in Uttar Pradesh, and from *Manda* in Jammu down to *Daimabad* in Maharashtra.

= 2. Subsistence Strategies & Agricultural Technology

Harappans lived on a wide range of domesticated plant and animal products.
Archaeologists reconstruct diet through archaeobotanical seeds and zooarchaeological animal bones.

== Plant Remains and Diets
- *Grains Found:* Grains included wheat, barley, lentil, chickpea, and sesame.
- *Millets:* Millet seeds are excavated predominantly from sites in *Gujarat*.
- *Rice Remains:* Finding rice grains was comparatively *rare* in mature sites.
- *Food Processing:* Saddle querns made of hard igneous rock or sandstone were used for grinding grain. Pounding querns had a cavity scooped out for spices.

== Animal Remains
- *Domestic Animals:* Bones of cattle, sheep, goat, buffalo, and pig indicate domestication.
- *Wild Species:* Bones of boar, deer, and gharial prove Harappans hunted or obtained meat from hunter communities.
- *Fish and Fowl:* Fish bones and bird remains are abundant along riverine and coastal outposts.

== Agricultural Tools and Water Management
Agriculture required sophisticated irrigation in semi-arid environments:

- *Plough Evidence:* A ploughed field showing two intersecting sets of furrows at right angles was discovered at *Kalibangan* in Rajasthan. This proves two different crops were grown together.
- *Terracotta Plough Models:* Clay models of ploughs have been unearthed at *Cholistan* and *Banawali* (Haryana).
- *Canal Traces:* Remnants of irrigation canals were found at *Shortughai* in northern Afghanistan, but not in Punjab or Sindh.
- *Water Storage:* Stone-cut *reservoirs* were discovered at *Dholavira* in Gujarat, used to harvest rainwater for agriculture and municipal use.

= 3. Mohenjodaro: A Planned Urban Centre

Mohenjodaro is the most celebrated Harappan city, illustrating masterful spatial planning.
The settlement was deliberately partitioned into two distinct physical sectors:

== The Citadel (Upper Settlement)
- *Position and Base:* Located on the western side. It was smaller in physical area but elevated on giant mud-brick platforms.
- *Walled Enclosure:* The Citadel was surrounded by brick defensive walls, separating it physically from the Lower Town.
- *Public Purpose:* It hosted monumental structures reserved for public rituals, state administration, and civic gatherings.
- *The Great Bath:* A vast rectangular tank set inside an open courtyard. It was made watertight using tightly fitted fired bricks laid on edge and sealed with *gypsum mortar*. Two flights of steps led down into the basin from the north and south. It was flanked by rooms on three sides, with one room housing a large brick well.
- *The Warehouse / Granary:* A massive brick foundation whose upper wooden superstructure decayed. It served as a centralized storage facility for state grain reserves.

== The Lower Town (Residential Sector)
- *Vast Residential Area:* Positioned to the east. It was larger, lower, and divided into neat residential blocks.
- *Walled Perimeters:* The Lower Town was also enclosed by outer brick walls.
- *Uniform Bricks:* All architectural construction used standardized bricks with a strict dimensional ratio of *1 : 2 : 4* (thickness : width : length). This uniform ratio was maintained across all Harappan territories.

== Street Grid and Municipal Drainage
- *Gridiron Pattern:* Roads and streets were laid out along an approximate *grid*, intersecting at *90-degree right angles*.
- *Streets First:* Streets and drainage ditches were laid out first, after which domestic houses were constructed along the grid.
- *Covered Channels:* Street drains were constructed of kiln-fired bricks or dressed limestone slabs. Slabs could be lifted for periodic desilting.
- *Soak Pits and Traps:* Domestic waste water passed through settling sumps where heavy solids settled, letting clear wastewater spill into street sewers.
- *Sanitary Standard:* The drainage system remains one of the most complete ancient sanitary works in human history.

== Domestic Architecture and Privacy
- *Courtyard Focus:* Domestic houses centered on an open courtyard. Rooms were arranged around its four edges.
- *Climatic Hub:* The courtyard served as the family centre for cooking and weaving during hot seasons.
- *Privacy Culture:* Ground-level exterior walls had *no windows*. The main entrance did not allow a direct line of sight into the interior courtyard.
- *Bathrooms & Wells:* Every house possessed an individual brick-paved bathroom. Drains connected directly through the wall into street channels. Mohenjodaro alone possessed approximately *700 brick wells*.

