= 4. Specialized Craft Production and Raw Material Procurement

Craft production formed the economic engine of Harappan urban life.

== Specialised Craft Centers
- *Chanhudaro:* A tiny settlement of less than 7 hectares, almost entirely devoted to craft production. Crafts included bead-making, seal-cutting, shell-working, and weight-manufacturing.
- *Coastal Shell Centers:* *Nageshwar* and *Balakot* were coastal settlements situated near natural molluscan colonies. They specialized in manufacturing shell bangles, ladles, and inlays.
- *Lothal:* A manufacturing hub in Gujarat. Excavations revealed a large bead factory, drill tools, and an expansive brick dockyard basin.

== Craft Materials and Technology
- *Stones:* Carnelian (a beautiful red stone), jasper, crystal, quartz, and *steatite*.
- *Metals:* Copper, bronze, and gold.
- *Other Media:* Terracotta, shell, and *faience* (a manufactured paste of crushed quartz and gum, glazed and fired into glossy pots).
- *Bead Techniques:* Harappans shaped beads into discs, cylinders, barrels, and spheres. Steatite micro-beads were made by moulding steatite powder paste. Carnelian got its deep red colour from firing yellowish raw pebbles. Specialized drill bits were recovered at Chanhudaro, Lothal, and Dholavira.

== Procurement Strategies and External Trade
Harappans secured distant raw materials through multiple channels:

